import Sql from '../../components/explainers/querying/Sql.astro';
import JoinFigure from '../../components/relational-next/a14/JoinFigure.astro';
import {setup, queries, orderValues, orderTotals, paymentTotals, stages, finalSelect, expectedReport} from '../../components/relational-next/a14/queries.mjs';
const headings = [
 ['row-contract','State what one result row represents'],
 ['matches','Follow the matches a join produces'],
 ['unmatched','Preserve unmatched rows deliberately'],
 ['grouping','Group at the level the question asks for'],
 ['counts','Make counts, missing values and conditional totals agree'],
 ['independent-totals','Combine independent totals without multiplying them'],
 ['proof','Prove the report against awkward data']
];
const conditional = `SELECT customer_id,
       SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'paid' THEN order_value ELSE 0 END) AS paid_value,
       SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'unpaid' THEN order_value ELSE 0 END) AS unpaid_value
FROM order_values
GROUP BY customer_id
ORDER BY customer_id;`;
const guard = `CASE WHEN COUNT(CASE WHEN status = 'paid' THEN 1 END)
             = COUNT(CASE WHEN status = 'paid' THEN order_value END)
     THEN SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'paid' THEN order_value ELSE 0 END)
END AS paid_value`;
---

<p>A join produces one row for each matching combination of source rows. An aggregate then works on those rows, including every copy the join produced. That is why a report can return the right customers, in the right order, with entirely wrong totals.</p>
<p>We will build one January customer report, including customers who did nothing in January. Each step keeps the meaning of a result row visible: first a customer paired with an order, then a customer’s group, and finally one customer with two independently calculated totals.</p>
<p>This follows the first join and grouped query in <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/">Working with SQL</a>. If you arrived here for a reporting problem, first read that article’s section <a href="/review/relational-next/sql/#checkable-stages">Express a larger question in checkable stages</a>. Its small staged query supplies the <code>WITH name AS (...)</code> notation used below. The examples here were executed in <strong>PostgreSQL 18.3 through PGlite 0.5.8</strong> and checked again in native PostgreSQL 18.6. Amounts use exact <code>numeric</code> values; dates are calendar dates, and the period includes 1 January 2026 and excludes 1 February.</p>

<nav aria-label="In this article"><ol>{headings.map(([id,title])=><li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<section aria-labelledby="row-contract">
 <h2 id="row-contract">State what one result row represents</h2>
 <p>The shop asks for <strong>one row per customer</strong>, identified by <code>customer_id</code>. It needs the number and value of orders placed in January, and the value of payments received in January. Every customer must appear, ordered by ID. These are two activity totals, not an outstanding-balance calculation: a January payment may settle an earlier order.</p>
 <p>An absent order or payment contributes zero. A line with an unknown amount is different: the order exists, but its complete value is unknown. That makes the customer’s complete order value unknown too. We will display that as <code>NULL</code>, alongside a non-zero order count, rather than quietly treating it as free.</p>
 <p>There are four tables. A customer has many orders and many payments. An order has many lines; its value is the sum of its lines. A payment belongs to a customer, with no direct relationship to an order in this example.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Report tables and their identities"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>One row represents</th><th>Key and reference</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><td><code>customers</code></td><td>One customer, whose name need not be unique</td><td><code>customer_id</code></td></tr>
   <tr><td><code>orders</code></td><td>One placed order</td><td><code>order_id</code>; <code>customer_id</code> references its customer</td></tr>
   <tr><td><code>order_lines</code></td><td>One line within an order</td><td><code>(order_id, line_no)</code>; <code>order_id</code> references its order</td></tr>
   <tr><td><code>payments</code></td><td>One receipt of money</td><td><code>payment_id</code>; <code>customer_id</code> references its customer</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>The fixture below is small enough to calculate without SQL. Two different customers are named Mina. Customer 1 has two January orders, with line values 20 + 10 and 20, and two separate payments of 15. Tariq’s only activity is in December. Edda has none. Sol has an order whose only line amount is unknown.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The complete report fixture in compact form"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Customer</th><th>Orders: date, status, line amounts</th><th>Payments: date, amount</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><td>1 · Mina</td><td>101: 10 Jan, paid, 20 + 10<br/>102: 20 Jan, unpaid, 20</td><td>201: 12 Jan, 15<br/>202: 13 Jan, 15</td></tr>
   <tr><td>2 · Tariq</td><td>103: 20 Dec 2025, paid, 90</td><td>205: 21 Dec 2025, 90</td></tr>
   <tr><td>3 · Mina</td><td>104: 15 Jan, paid, 40</td><td>203: 16 Jan, 10</td></tr>
   <tr><td>4 · Edda</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr>
   <tr><td>5 · Sol</td><td>105: 18 Jan, paid, NULL</td><td>204: 19 Jan, 5</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Before writing a join, calculate customer 1’s expected row: <code>(1, Mina, 2, 50, 30)</code>. Edda’s is <code>(4, Edda, 0, 0, 0)</code>. Sol’s is <code>(5, Sol, 1, NULL, 5)</code>. These three rows test multiplication, preservation and unknown values separately.</p>
 <details><summary>Create the complete disposable fixture</summary><p>Run this once in a fresh PostgreSQL session. The temporary tables disappear when the session ends. Every date without a year in the table above is in 2026.</p><Sql code={setup} label="Temporary tables and all fixture rows" /></details>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="matches">
 <h2 id="matches">Follow the matches a join produces</h2>
 <p>The first query asks a smaller question: which customer placed each January order? <code>c</code> and <code>o</code> are independent aliases for the two table references. The join condition pairs an order’s customer reference with the customer’s key.</p>
 <Sql code={queries.pairs} label="One row per customer and January order pair" />
 <p>Predict the number of rows before you play Figure 1. Customer 1 matches two orders, customers 3 and 5 one each. Then remove the relationship condition and watch what changes.</p>
 <JoinFigure scene="matches" number={1} />
 <p>With the key condition, the four rows are <code>(1, Mina, 101)</code>, <code>(1, Mina, 102)</code>, <code>(3, Mina, 104)</code> and <code>(5, Sol, 105)</code>. Customer 1 appears twice because two source pairs satisfy the condition. That is useful repetition if one result row means one order; it is a bug if the caller expects one customer.</p>
 <p><strong>Cardinality</strong> is the number of rows involved. A one-to-one match contributes at most one right-hand row per left-hand row. A one-to-many match can contribute several. When several rows on each side share the matching value, every left match pairs with every right match: two against three means six pairs. In an inner equality join, a key value occurring <em>m</em> times on one side and <em>n</em> on the other contributes <em>m × n</em> rows. A missing value on either side contributes none.</p>
 <p>No condition at all gives every possible pair, a <code>CROSS JOIN</code>. Figure 1’s five customers and four January orders become twenty rows. A condition such as <code>ON true</code> does the same thing. More subtle mistakes leave a condition present but incomplete.</p>

 <h3 id="incomplete-key">Use the whole relationship</h3>
 <p>Suppose an imported system numbers orders within each tenant. Its order key is <code>(tenant_id, order_no)</code>, and both tenant 1 and tenant 2 have order 7. Joining lines on order number alone gives four pairs for two orders, including each tenant’s order paired with the other tenant’s line. Both parts of the key belong in the predicate:</p>
 <Sql code={queries.composite} label="Two tenant-scoped order identities, matched in full" />
 <p>This returns two rows, <code>(1, 7, 1)</code> and <code>(2, 7, 2)</code>. Remove <code>l.tenant_id = o.tenant_id</code> and it returns four. A foreign key can enforce the full reference in storage; the query must still express the full relationship.</p>
 <p>A matching column need not be unique just because its name sounds identifying. Joining by a customer’s display name would merge our two Minas. Check the declared keys and the actual matches. When an unexpected count first appears after a join, list the source keys before adding aggregation: the extra pairs usually explain it.</p>

 <h3 id="self-join">Give each role its own alias</h3>
 <p>The same pairing rule applies when both references name the same table. To list employees with their managers, <code>e</code> stands for the employee and <code>m</code> for the manager. Their aliases name roles, not copies of stored data. Here a tiny inline fixture keeps the example separate from the shop:</p>
 <Sql code={queries.self} label="An employee and their manager are two roles in one join" />
 <p>The rows are <code>(Mina, NULL)</code> and <code>(Tariq, Mina)</code>. The left join retains Mina, who has no manager. An inner join would omit her. Traversing a whole management hierarchy is a different task.</p>
 <p>If the question is only “which customers placed any January order?”, use the parent article’s presence test. <code>EXISTS</code> answers yes or no for each customer; it does not attach one output row per order, so customer 1 appears once:</p>
 <Sql code={queries.presence} label="Presence selects customers without attaching their matching orders" />
 <p>The result contains customers 1, 3 and 5. <a href="/review/relational-next/query-composition/">Composing larger SQL queries</a> develops existence and membership tests. The pairing drawings here explain the answer’s meaning; <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a> explains how an engine finds those pairs.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="unmatched">
 <h2 id="unmatched">Preserve unmatched rows deliberately</h2>
 <p>Our report needs every customer, so orders must be optional. A <code>LEFT JOIN</code> first produces matching pairs. For each left-hand row with no match, it supplies one result row with NULLs for the right-hand columns. That is a <strong>null-extended row</strong>.</p>
 <p>“No match” depends on the whole <code>ON</code> condition. Put the period there, and Tariq has no qualifying order, just like Edda. Put it in <code>WHERE</code>, and Tariq first matches his December order, then loses that row to the date test. The join never supplies an extra row for him afterwards.</p>
 <Sql code={queries.outerOn} label="Qualify orders in ON while retaining every customer" />
 <p>Step through Figure 2 with the condition in <em>ON</em>, then move it to <em>WHERE</em>. Watch Tariq and Edda at the filtering step.</p>
 <JoinFigure scene="outer" number={2} />
 <details><summary>The query with the date condition moved to WHERE</summary><Sql code={queries.outerWhere} label="A later filter rejects December and null-extended rows" /></details>
 <p>In <code>WHERE</code>, a row stays only when its condition is true. The date comparison is false for Tariq’s December order and unknown for Edda’s null-extended date, so both disappear. The result now means “customers with January orders”. That can be the intended question, but it breaks this report’s contract.</p>
 <p>Adding <code>OR o.order_id IS NULL</code> to that WHERE clause rescues Edda, but not Tariq: his matched December order has a non-null ID. Moving the qualification into ON expresses the intended question directly.</p>
 <p>Use <code>o.order_id IS NULL</code> when you need to recognise “no qualifying order”, because a stored order’s primary key cannot be null. Sol’s <code>order_lines.amount IS NULL</code> means something else: a real matched line has an unknown amount. Testing a nullable attribute for absence confuses these two cases. <a href="/review/relational-next/sql-values/">Getting SQL values right</a> explains the missing-value logic behind the date test.</p>
 <p>A right join preserves the table on its right; swapping table order lets you express the same question with a left join. A full join preserves unmatched rows from both sides. Neither is needed here, because customers define the complete list we must retain.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="grouping">
 <h2 id="grouping">Group at the level the question asks for</h2>
 <p>Grouping replaces each group of input rows with one result row. To produce one row per customer, group by the customer’s identity. Keep the display name alongside it explicitly. This query selects customers with at least two January orders:</p>
 <Sql code={queries.groups} label="Filter completed customer groups with HAVING" />
 <p>It returns <code>(1, Mina, 2)</code>. Grouping by <code>c.name</code> alone would combine both Minas and report three orders. Adding <code>o.status</code> instead would split customer 1 into paid and unpaid groups. An added grouping column can split a group when its values vary within it. A name determined by the customer ID does not split that customer's group; status can. Choose grouping columns for the required result, rather than just to make a SELECT error disappear.</p>
 <p>What if you also select <code>o.placed_on</code>? Customer 1 has two dates, and the group has no single answer. Choose a rule: <code>MIN(o.placed_on)</code> for the first, <code>MAX</code> for the last, or include the date in the grouping keys if one row per customer and date is what you need.</p>
 <p>PostgreSQL can allow an ungrouped table column when that table’s primary key is grouped: the key determines its other columns. Other engines recognise different dependencies; SQLite even allows “bare” ungrouped columns in cases PostgreSQL rejects. Explicit grouping columns and deliberate aggregates make the intended result clear across engines. Do not infer a meaningful arbitrary date from a query that happens to run.</p>
 <p><code>WHERE</code> filters individual source rows before grouping. <code>HAVING</code> filters completed groups, as the count threshold does here. Filtering orders to “paid” in WHERE and then counting them answers a different question from counting all orders and retaining customers whose paid count is large enough.</p>
 <p>The final <code>ORDER BY</code> is part of the contract too. Neither grouping nor a join promises an output order. To keep each order row while adding a customer total beside it, use a window calculation rather than collapsing the orders into a group; see <a href="/review/relational-next/window-functions/">Calculating across related rows</a>.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="counts">
 <h2 id="counts">Make counts, missing values and conditional totals agree</h2>
 <p>On an outer join, “count the rows” is ambiguous. Count the joined rows, the real detail rows, or the known measured values? This query exposes all three, plus the number of different measured amounts:</p>
 <Sql code={queries.counts} label="Four counts over the same customer, order and line rows" />
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Counts measured in PostgreSQL"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Customer</th><th>COUNT(*)</th><th>COUNT(line_no)</th><th>COUNT(amount)</th><th>COUNT(DISTINCT amount)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td>1 · Mina</td><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>2 · Tariq</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>3 · Mina</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>4 · Edda</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>5 · Sol</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p><code>COUNT(*)</code> counts every input row, including a null-extended row. <code>COUNT(l.line_no)</code> counts real lines because the line key is never null in storage. <code>COUNT(l.amount)</code> counts only known amounts, so Sol has one line but zero known amounts. <code>COUNT(DISTINCT l.amount)</code> counts different non-null values: customer 1’s three lines contain only two amounts, 10 and 20. It counts neither lines nor orders.</p>
 <p>In PostgreSQL, <code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>MIN</code> and <code>MAX</code> ignore null input values and return NULL if no non-null input remains. That policy can hide incomplete measurements. Adding 20 and NULL gives a SUM of 20, but the complete total is unknown under our report’s rules. Check completeness as well as the sum.</p>
 <p>We first calculate one value per January order. A missing line set, or any line with an unknown amount, makes that order’s complete value unknown:</p>
 <Sql code={orderValues+';'} label="One row per January order, with completeness preserved" />
 <p>This returns order values <code>101 → 30</code>, <code>102 → 20</code>, <code>104 → 40</code> and <code>105 → NULL</code>. The zero-line guard also covers an order stored before its lines arrive. The fixture’s foreign keys do not require every order to have a line.</p>

 <h3 id="empty-input">No rows, or a row with no details?</h3>
 <p>A global aggregate without GROUP BY returns one row even when nothing qualifies. A grouped query with no input has no groups to return. These are observed PostgreSQL results:</p>
 <Sql code={queries.empty} label="Empty input, one aggregate row: n = 0, total = NULL" />
 <Sql code={queries.noGroups} label="Empty input with GROUP BY: zero result rows" />
 <p>The retained customers in the earlier left join are another case: they supply rows even when no detail matches. Edda therefore has <code>COUNT(*) = 1</code> and <code>COUNT(l.line_no) = 0</code>. The report must turn that absence into zero without turning Sol’s unknown amount into zero.</p>
 <p><code>COALESCE(SUM(amount), 0)</code> is appropriate only when every reason for a null total should mean zero. Our payments have non-null amounts, so an absent payment subtotal can default to zero. Order totals can be null because a real order is unvalued; they need a separate missing-match test. The final query will make that distinction.</p>

 <h3 id="conditional-totals">Put the condition inside the aggregate</h3>
 <p>The shop also asks how much order value is marked paid and how much unpaid. Here “paid” is the stored order status; it does not allocate the payment receipts to orders. Using the <code>order_values</code> stage above, the basic calculation is:</p>
 <Sql code={conditional} label="Separate status totals without removing orders from the input" />
 <p>For customer 1, the CASE expressions contribute <code>(30, 0)</code> from order 101 and <code>(0, 20)</code> from order 102. SUM gives paid value 30 and unpaid value 20. A WHERE condition on status would remove one category from both aggregates.</p>
 <p>The simple form still ignores unknown order values. If a customer had one known paid order and one unknown paid order, it could publish an incomplete paid total. Compare the number of paid orders with the number of known paid values before returning their sum:</p>
 <Sql code={guard} label="Return an unknown paid total when any paid order is unvalued" />
 <p>The first COUNT counts every paid order; the second counts only paid orders with a known value. Apply the same guard to unpaid orders. The complete checked version returns customer 1’s <code>(30, 20)</code>, customer 3’s <code>(40, 0)</code> and Sol’s <code>(NULL, 0)</code>. Customers with no orders still need the final left join to appear.</p>
 <details><summary>Complete conditional-total query</summary><Sql code={queries.conditional} label="Paid and unpaid values with completeness checks" /></details>
 <p>PostgreSQL also supports <code>SUM(order_value) FILTER (WHERE status = 'paid')</code>. FILTER restricts the input of that aggregate alone; it still needs the same decision about empty and unknown inputs. <a href="/review/relational-next/sql-values/">Getting SQL values right</a> develops CASE, defaults and expression arithmetic.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="independent-totals">
 <h2 id="independent-totals">Combine independent totals without multiplying them</h2>
 <p>We now have one row per order, so it is tempting to join those orders and the payments to customers, then group by customer. For customer 1, predict the two sums before playing Figure 3. The source sets contain orders 101 = 30 and 102 = 20, and payments 201 = 15 and 202 = 15.</p>
 <JoinFigure scene="fanout" number={3} />
 <p>The join emits four combinations: 101 with 201, 101 with 202, 102 with 201, and 102 with 202. SUM sees order values <code>30, 30, 20, 20</code> and payment amounts <code>15, 15, 15, 15</code>. It returns <strong>100 and 60</strong>, twice the correct 50 and 30. Grouping all four rows under one customer changes their presentation, not their contributions.</p>
 <details><summary>The plausible but incorrect report</summary><Sql code={queries.wrong} label="Wrong: two independent one-to-many joins before aggregation" /></details>
 <p>Switch Figure 3 to <em>Total each side first</em>. The repair is to make each independent input agree with the final row contract before joining them. Aggregate order values at customer level; aggregate payments separately at customer level. Each intermediate result now has at most one row per customer:</p>
 <Sql code={orderTotals+';'} label="The order_totals stage, reading order_values" />
 <Sql code={paymentTotals+';'} label="The independent payment_totals stage" />
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Intermediate totals checked in PostgreSQL"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Customer ID</th><th>order_totals: count, value</th><th>payment_totals: value</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>2, 50</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>No row</td><td>No row</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1, 40</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>No row</td><td>No row</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1, NULL</td><td>5</td></tr></tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>“No row” is shown for comparison; it is not a stored null subtotal. Each GROUP BY produces only customer IDs with qualifying input. The final query starts from customers and left joins both results. An absent order subtotal becomes zero; a present subtotal with an unknown value stays unknown.</p>
 <Sql code={finalSelect+';'} label="Final SELECT after defining the three stages" />
 <details><summary>Complete report, ready to run after the fixture</summary><Sql code={stages+'\n'+finalSelect+';'} label="The complete January report" /></details>
 <p>The row-count guarantee is structural. A customer key is unique in customers. Grouping order_totals and payment_totals by that same key makes it unique in each of those results too. Joining a customer to at most one row from each side cannot multiply its final row.</p>
 <p><code>SELECT DISTINCT</code> on the final grouped rows cannot help: there is already one wrong row per customer. <code>SUM(DISTINCT amount)</code> removes equal values rather than repeated source identities. Customer 1’s two legitimate payments both equal 15, so it would return 15 instead of 30. A distinct order count may repair that count, but leaves the unrelated payment sum wrong. Fix where duplication enters; do not ask an aggregate to guess which copies were legitimate.</p>
 <p>The report needs no subtotal rows. Adding an “all customers” row to the same data would create a second level of detail, and a later SUM over every row would count its contribution again. Keep the separately checked overall totals outside these customer rows.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="proof">
 <h2 id="proof">Prove the report against awkward data</h2>
 <p>The complete hand-calculated expectation is below. PostgreSQL returned these same five rows, in this order:</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Expected and observed January customer report"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>customer_id</th><th>name</th><th>order_count</th><th>order_value</th><th>payment_value</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{expectedReport.map(row=><tr><td>{row.customer_id}</td><td>{row.name}</td><td>{row.order_count}</td><td>{row.order_value===null?'NULL':Number(row.order_value)}</td><td>{Number(row.payment_value)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Checking only the final row count would pass the multiplied report. Checking only Mina’s totals would miss the dropped inactive customers. Compare complete rows, including nulls, and then check independent facts:</p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Identity:</strong> five rows, five distinct customer IDs, with both Minas separate. Group each intermediate result by customer_id and look for <code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code>; neither may return a row.</li>
  <li><strong>Value:</strong> four January orders, three fully valued, with known values totalling 90. January payments total 45, calculated directly from payments without an order join. The customer rows must agree with both calculations.</li>
  <li><strong>Completeness:</strong> one customer has an unknown complete order value. A SUM of the customer values alone returns 90 by ignoring Sol; that is a known-value subtotal, not the complete overall order total. The complete total remains unknown.</li>
  <li><strong>Period and order:</strong> Tariq’s December activity contributes zero. The explicit final ORDER BY returns IDs 1 through 5. Changing both period bounds to February retains all five customers with zero counts and values.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>The fixture makes each failure observable. Customer 1 catches several details on both sides and repeated legitimate amounts; customers 1 and 3 catch grouping by name; Sol catches a nullable measured value; Tariq and Edda distinguish out-of-period activity from none. The empty-input queries establish the difference between a global aggregate and no groups.</p>
 <p>Two intentional changes were executed as negative checks. Joining the unaggregated detail sets produced Mina’s wrong <code>(100, 60)</code> totals. Moving the order period condition from ON to WHERE removed Tariq and Edda. The shared ID and value columns exposed the wrong sums; comparing the ON and WHERE customer/order pairs exposed the dropped customers. Removing Sol’s only line also left the order value unknown, confirming the zero-line guard.</p>
 <p>These are correctness checks on a small fixture. They establish the result meaning, not acceptable execution cost. Once the answer is right, use representative data and the method in <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a> to inspect the work needed to produce it.</p>
</section>

<section class="essay-sources" aria-labelledby="sources">
 <h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
 <p>Checked on 19 October 2026. The fixture is invented. Reported SQL results were checked in PGlite 0.5.8, PostgreSQL 18.3, and native PostgreSQL 18.6, with the same answers. The interactive figures calculate illustrative logical steps in JavaScript; they do not execute SQL or show the engine’s physical join order. SQLite’s grouping allowance is documentation-based, not a SQLite run.</p>
 <ul>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/tutorial-join.html">Joins Between Tables</a>: matching pairs, aliases, self-joins and preserved rows.</li>
